## Abstract

This paper presents a formal, cross-disciplinary diagnosis of idol worship as the recurrent structural error of mistaking human artifacts for natural facts. Drawing on cognitive science, epistemology, psychology, and systems theory, we demonstrate that idol worship is not a religious anomaly but a fundamental cognitive bias that repeats across cultures, eras, and domains. We show that this error is reinforced by the "epistemology of error" (Allchin, 2001)—the tendency to treat published observations as facts while suppressing contradictory evidence —and by the "Pila Effect" (Saadat, 2026), wherein verifying a small detail creates an illusion of truth for a larger false narrative . The historical pattern is traced from ancient Mesopotamian cult statues  and Biblical idol polemics  to contemporary logical and technological idols. We conclude that fidelity—the recognition of the gap between artifact and fact—is the only appropriate response to this structural error.

