## 2. The Epistemology of Error: How Artifacts Become Facts

Douglas Allchin's work on the epistemology of error reveals a critical mechanism: the "fact/artifact distinction" needs to be supplemented with a more fundamental "resolved/uncertain distinction" . Errors in identification are perpetuated through a psychological chain reaction: the first observer mistakes a flat image for the object itself; the verbal description supersedes visual evidence; the printed word immunizes the reader against critical evaluation; and the reader regards a published opinion as a "fact" .

This mechanism explains why artifacts become facts: the authority of the printed word, reinforced by the cognitive bias of accepting published observations, transforms human creations into perceived grounds of truth.

The "Pila Effect" (Saadat, 2026) provides a complementary cognitive mechanism: verifying a small, easily checkable detail creates an illusion of truth for a larger, often false narrative . This is the cognitive architecture that transforms a single accurate observation into a framework of false belief.

