## 3. The Historical Pattern: From Cult Statues to Logical Idols

### 3.1 Ancient Idolatry: The Work of Human Hands

The Biblical idol polemics (Psalm 115, Isaiah 17, Jeremiah 51) provide a consistent diagnosis: idols are "the work of human hands" . Archaeological finds—the Lachish Ostraca, Ugaritic cultic figurines, Babylonian votive figurines—verify the widespread worship of human-made objects . The Mesopotamian Mīs Pî ritual, which "enlivened" cult statues, demonstrates the cognitive process by which an inanimate object is believed to become a deity .

Cognitive science reveals the mechanism: humans naturally attribute agency to objects . This intuitive cognition, when combined with costly rituals, produces the belief that an artifact is the divine. The Jewish Encyclopedia confirms that "at first the gods and their images were conceived of as identical" .

### 3.2 The Pattern of Collapse

The historical pattern is consistent:

| Era | Idol | Mistaken For | Collapse |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Ancient** | Religious statues | Gods | Empires fell |
| **Classical** | Philosophical systems | Ground of truth | Schools dissolved |
| **Modern** | Logic | Ground of reason | Systems failing |
| **Contemporary** | Technology/AGI | Ground of progress | Global crisis |

Jeremiah's prophecy against Babylon (Jeremiah 51:18) was historically vindicated: Babylon capitulated despite its opulent temples and thousands of votive figurines . The pattern is consistent: artifacts cannot save in crisis.

