## 5. The Contemporary Application: Logical and Technological Idols

Modern cognitive science confirms that the pattern persists. McCutcheon & Maltby (2017) link intense celebrity worship to anxiety and depression, mirroring the ancient warning that trust in powerless objects dehumanizes . Kahneman's "WYSIATI" bias—"what you see is all there is"—reveals that our minds mistake coherence for truth, ignoring the quality and quantity of information .

The "illusion of control" (Langer, 1975) drives the worship of technological idols: we believe we can master reality through tools . The functional fixedness described by Duncker (1945)—our inability to see objects in new ways—reinforces our attachment to existing idols .

The current idol is logic and its technological derivatives. Logic, a human artifact, is treated as the ground of truth. AGI, a theoretical artifact, is treated as the source of progress. The pattern is identical to ancient idol worship: the work of human hands is mistaken for the ground of reality.

