## 7. Conclusion

The historical pattern of idol worship is a structural error: the systematic confusion of artifacts with facts. This error is reinforced by cognitive biases—the "epistemology of error," the "Pila Effect," WYSIATI—and institutional authority. It repeats across cultures and eras, from ancient cult statues to contemporary logical and technological idols.

The polymath diagnosis integrates multiple disciplines to reveal the error. The only appropriate response is fidelity: the recognition of the gap between artifact and fact, and the commitment to serve reality rather than worship human creations.

The pursuit continues—in fidelity.

